    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d194">

                <pb facs="0401" n="387" sameAs="frus:frus1944v01/pg387"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 EAC/11–744: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chargé in the
                        United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gallman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-11-07T23:00:00-04:00">November 7,
                            1944—11 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>9325. Reurtels 9442 and 9261.<anchor n="50" xml:id="d194-f1"/> As you are aware,
                    questions of economic policy occupied a major place in Cabinet Committee
                    deliberations regarding treatment of Germany, but full clarification of policy
                    was not obtained. Following these deliberations, the Department was recently
                    asked to furnish an amplification of its views regarding economic policy toward
                    Germany. Material along these lines is now in course of preparation and will be
                    submitted shortly. In this way we hope to obtain sufficient clarification of
                    basic policy on economic treatment of Germany to enable us to begin discussions
                    with British and Russians on reparation and economic security. At this stage,
                    however, we do not believe that any workable distinction between policy matters
                    and technical matters can be drawn. Consequently, we wish, despite our earlier
                    proposals, to defer tripartite conversations until we have obtained definite
                    clearance on basic lines of policy. We appreciate present urgency of getting
                    ahead on conversations on these subjects, and we believe that necessary policy
                    clearance can be obtained in a few weeks.</p>
                <p>Sent to London, repeated to Moscow re its tel 4037.<anchor n="51"
                        xml:id="d194-f2"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
